d = B.d

# ---------------- HTML / CSS ----------------
d("web_css", "How do I center a div horizontally and vertically?", "The simplest modern way is flexbox on the parent:\n\n```css\n.parent {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */\n  align-items: center;     /* vertical */\n  min-height: 100vh;\n}\n```\n\nGrid works too: `display: grid; place-items: center;`. The parent needs a height (here `min-height`) or there is nothing to center within.", diff="easy")
d("web_css", "my flex items overflow the container instead of wrapping", "Flex items stay on one line by default. Add `flex-wrap: wrap` to the container. If a child still overflows, it is probably wider than the container because of a fixed `width` or long unbroken text; set `min-width: 0` on the flex child (so it may shrink below its content size) and `overflow-wrap: anywhere` for long strings.\n\n```css\n.row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; }\n.row > .item { flex: 1 1 200px; min-width: 0; }\n```", diff="medium")
d("web_css", "What's the difference between margin and padding?", "Padding is space inside an element, between its content and its border (the background shows through it). Margin is space outside the border, separating the element from its neighbours (it is transparent). Vertical margins between block siblings can collapse into the larger of the two; padding never collapses.", diff="easy")
d("web_css", "CSS specificity - why is my class being ignored?\n\n```css\n#nav a { color: grey; }\n.link { color: red; }\n```\n```html\n<nav id=\"nav\"><a class=\"link\">Home</a></nav>\n```", "Specificity decides, not order. `#nav a` has an id (1,0,0) plus an element (0,0,1), while `.link` has only a class (0,1,0), so the grey rule wins. Fix it by lowering the first selector's specificity, for example `nav a`, or by matching it: `#nav .link { color: red; }`. Avoid `!important`; it makes later overrides harder.", diff="medium")
d("web_css", "When should I use grid and when flexbox?", "Use flexbox for one-dimensional layouts: a row of buttons, a navbar, items distributed along a line. Use grid for two-dimensional layouts where rows and columns must line up, like a page layout or a card gallery. They combine well: a grid for the page, flexbox inside each card.\n\n```css\n.gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; }\n```", diff="easy")
d("web_css", "How do I make a layout responsive without writing lots of media queries?", "Lean on fluid techniques first: relative units, `clamp()` for type, and `auto-fit`/`minmax` grids that reflow on their own.\n\n```css\n.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 260px), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }\nh1 { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.5rem); }\nimg { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }\n```\n\nAdd a media query only where the design really needs to change (for example, switching a sidebar to a top bar). Don't forget `<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">`.", diff="medium")
d("web_a11y", "What makes a form accessible?", "Every input needs a programmatic label, errors must be announced, and the form must work with a keyboard.\n\n```html\n<label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n<input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"email\" required aria-describedby=\"email-err\">\n<p id=\"email-err\" role=\"alert\"></p>\n```\n\nUse real `<button>`s, keep visible focus styles, don't rely on colour alone to show errors, and don't use placeholder text as the only label.", diff="medium")
d("web_a11y", "Is it fine to use a div with an onClick as a button?", "It's better to use `<button type=\"button\">`. A `div` isn't focusable, doesn't react to Enter or Space, and has no button role for screen readers; you would have to add `tabindex`, `role=\"button\"` and key handlers yourself. A native button gives all of that for free, and you can restyle it fully with CSS.", diff="easy")
d("web_a11y", "What should alt text say?", "Describe the image's purpose in context, briefly. A product photo: \"Red running shoe, side view\". A chart: say the main takeaway, with data in text nearby. Purely decorative images get an empty `alt=\"\"` so screen readers skip them. Don't start with \"image of\", and don't leave out the attribute entirely.", diff="easy")
d("web_html", "What's the point of semantic HTML tags?", "Tags like `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, `<footer>` and `<button>` tell browsers, assistive technology and search engines what each part is. Screen-reader users can jump between landmarks, keyboard behaviour comes built in, and your CSS and tests get clearer hooks than a sea of `<div>`s.", diff="easy")
d("web_html", "Why does my page look zoomed out on phones?", "Without a viewport meta tag, mobile browsers render the page at a desktop width (about 980px) and scale it down. Add this to `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n```\n\nThen make sure your CSS uses flexible widths rather than fixed pixel widths.", diff="easy")
d("web_html", "defer vs async on script tags?", "Both download the script without blocking HTML parsing. `defer` runs scripts after the document is parsed, in the order they appear, which suits most app code. `async` runs each script as soon as it arrives, in any order, which suits independent scripts like analytics. Scripts without either block parsing while they download and execute.", diff="medium")
d("web_css", "Why doesn't `z-index` work on my element?", "`z-index` only affects positioned elements (anything except `position: static`), flex or grid items, and it only competes within its stacking context. If a parent creates its own stacking context (via `position` with `z-index`, `transform`, `opacity < 1`, `filter` and similar), a child can never rise above elements outside that parent, no matter how large its z-index. Inspect the ancestors, and give the element `position: relative` if it has none.", diff="hard")
d("web_css", "How do I truncate long text with an ellipsis?", "For one line:\n\n```css\n.title { white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }\n```\n\nFor multiple lines, use the line-clamp pattern, which is widely supported:\n\n```css\n.excerpt { display: -webkit-box; -webkit-line-clamp: 3; -webkit-box-orient: vertical; overflow: hidden; }\n```\n\nIn a flex child you may also need `min-width: 0`.", diff="medium")
d("web_css", "Light/dark mode with CSS only?", "Define colours as custom properties and override them in a media query:\n\n```css\n:root { --bg: #ffffff; --fg: #1a1a1a; }\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\n  :root { --bg: #121212; --fg: #eaeaea; }\n}\nbody { background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg); }\n```\n\nIf you also offer a manual toggle, set a `data-theme` attribute and put the overrides under `:root[data-theme=\"dark\"]` as well.", diff="medium")
d("web_css", "This button's hover style doesn't show on touch devices. Is that a bug?", "It's expected: touch screens have no real hover, and many browsers emulate it with a sticky state after a tap. Don't hide important information behind `:hover`. Use `@media (hover: hover)` to apply hover-only styling on devices that support it, and give `:focus-visible` and `:active` states as well.", diff="medium")
d("web_css", "What does `box-sizing: border-box` do?", "By default (`content-box`) the `width` you set covers only the content, so padding and borders make the element wider. With `border-box`, the width includes padding and border, which makes layout arithmetic predictable. Most projects set it globally:\n\n```css\n*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }\n```", diff="easy")
d("web_css", "I have a long table that breaks my mobile layout. Options?", "Wrap it in a container that scrolls horizontally, which keeps the table semantics:\n\n```css\n.table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }\n.table-wrap table { min-width: 600px; }\n```\n\nFor a few columns, you can instead reflow rows into stacked cards at small widths with CSS, but keep real table markup for screen readers. Hide low-priority columns only if the information is not essential.", diff="medium")
d("web_html", "Review this HTML.\n\n```html\n<div class=\"menu\">\n  <div onclick=\"go('/home')\">Home</div>\n  <div onclick=\"go('/about')\">About</div>\n</div>\n<img src=\"hero.jpg\">\n```", "Issues, most important first:\n\n1. Navigation items are clickable `div`s: not focusable, no link semantics, no middle-click or open-in-new-tab. Use real links inside `<nav>`.\n2. The image lacks an `alt` attribute.\n3. Inline `onclick` mixes behaviour into markup.\n\n```html\n<nav aria-label=\"Main\">\n  <a href=\"/home\">Home</a>\n  <a href=\"/about\">About</a>\n</nav>\n<img src=\"hero.jpg\" alt=\"Team working at a whiteboard\" width=\"1200\" height=\"600\">\n```\n\nExplicit `width` and `height` also reduce layout shift while the image loads.", diff="medium")

# ---------------- React ----------------
d("react_hooks", "My counter in React only goes up by 1 even though I call setCount(count + 1) three times:\n\n```jsx\nfunction add3() {\n  setCount(count + 1);\n  setCount(count + 1);\n  setCount(count + 1);\n}\n```", "Each call reads the same `count` from the current render, so all three schedule `count + 1` and you get +1. Use the functional form, which receives the latest pending state:\n\n```jsx\nfunction add3() {\n  setCount(c => c + 1);\n  setCount(c => c + 1);\n  setCount(c => c + 1);\n}\n```\n\nThat yields +3. Use the updater form whenever the new state depends on the previous one.", diff="medium")
d("react_hooks", "Why does my setInterval in useEffect always log the old state?\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const id = setInterval(() => console.log(count), 1000);\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\n}, []);\n```", "That's a stale closure. The effect ran once, so the callback captured `count` from the first render and never sees later values. Options:\n\n1. Put `count` in the dependency array so the interval is recreated when it changes (fine for logging).\n2. Keep the latest value in a ref: `const latest = useRef(count); latest.current = count;` and read `latest.current` inside the interval.\n3. If you only update state, use the updater form (`setCount(c => c + 1)`) so no captured value is needed.\n\nThe `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` lint rule flags exactly this mistake.", diff="hard")
d("react_hooks", "What should go in the useEffect dependency array?", "Every reactive value (props, state, and variables derived from them) that the effect reads. React compares them between renders and re-runs the effect when one changes. An empty array means \"run once after mount\", which is only correct if the effect truly uses no reactive values. Don't silence the lint rule; instead restructure: move functions inside the effect, use `useCallback` for stable callbacks, or use the updater form of `setState`. Objects and arrays created inline in the component are new on every render, so depending on them re-runs the effect each time.", diff="medium")
d("react_hooks", "My useEffect causes an infinite loop:\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  setItems([...items, 'x']);\n}, [items]);\n```", "The effect depends on `items` and also changes `items`, so every run triggers another render and another run. Ask what should trigger the update. If it should happen once, use `[]` with the updater form:\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  setItems(prev => [...prev, 'x']);\n}, []);\n```\n\nIf the value is derived from other state, compute it during render instead of storing it in state plus an effect.", diff="medium")
d("react_hooks", "Do I need useEffect to fetch data? Is this OK?\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  fetch(`/api/users/${id}`).then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);\n}, [id]);\n```", "It works but has two problems. If `id` changes quickly, responses can arrive out of order and an old response overwrites the newer one. There is also no error or loading handling. Cancel the stale request:\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const ctrl = new AbortController();\n  fetch(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal: ctrl.signal })\n    .then(r => { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(r.status); return r.json(); })\n    .then(setUser)\n    .catch(e => { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') setError(e); });\n  return () => ctrl.abort();\n}, [id]);\n```\n\nFor real apps, a data-fetching library (such as TanStack Query) gives caching, retries and deduplication so you write far less of this by hand.", diff="hard")
d("react_keys", "React warns 'Each child in a list should have a unique key'. What is it and what should I use?", "Keys let React match list items between renders, so it can reorder, insert and remove items correctly and keep their state. Use a stable unique identifier from your data, such as a database id:\n\n```jsx\n{todos.map(t => <TodoItem key={t.id} todo={t} />)}\n```\n\nAvoid the array index if the list can be reordered, filtered or inserted into: items then keep the wrong state (like input text). Never generate keys with `Math.random()` during render, because they change every time and force remounting.", diff="easy")
d("react_forms", "How do controlled inputs work in React?", "A controlled input gets its value from state and reports changes through a handler, so React is the source of truth:\n\n```jsx\nfunction Name() {\n  const [name, setName] = useState('');\n  return <input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />;\n}\n```\n\nIf you set `value` without `onChange`, the field becomes read-only and React warns. Use `defaultValue` for an uncontrolled input whose value you read on submit (with a ref or `FormData`).", diff="easy")
d("react_state", "Two sibling components need the same data. Where does the state go?", "Lift it to their closest common parent and pass it down as props, with callbacks for changes:\n\n```jsx\nfunction Parent() {\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\n  return (\n    <>\n      <SearchBox value={query} onChange={setQuery} />\n      <Results query={query} />\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nIf the state must travel through many layers, consider context or a state library, but start with lifting; it is the simplest option and keeps data flow explicit.", diff="medium")
d("react_perf", "When should I use React.memo, useMemo and useCallback?", "Not by default. React re-rendering is usually cheap. Reach for them when you have measured a problem (the Profiler shows slow renders), typically:\n\n- `React.memo` on a component that is expensive to render and often receives the same props.\n- `useMemo` for an expensive calculation, or to keep an object/array prop referentially stable for a memoised child.\n- `useCallback` for a function passed to a memoised child or used in an effect's dependencies.\n\nMemoisation has its own cost and adds complexity. Often it is better to move state down, or split a component, so fewer things re-render.", diff="hard")
d("react_perf", "Why does my memoized child still re-render?\n\n```jsx\nconst Child = React.memo(function Child({ onSave }) { ... });\nfunction Parent() {\n  return <Child onSave={() => save()} />;\n}\n```", "`React.memo` compares props shallowly, and the arrow function `() => save()` is a new function on every render of `Parent`, so the prop is always \"different\". Stabilise it with `useCallback`:\n\n```jsx\nconst onSave = useCallback(() => save(), []);\nreturn <Child onSave={onSave} />;\n```\n\nList every value the function uses in the dependency array. The same applies to inline objects and arrays passed as props.", diff="hard")
d("react_state", "Is it a problem that I mutate state directly?\n\n```jsx\nconst [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);\nfunction add(t) {\n  todos.push(t);\n  setTodos(todos);\n}\n```", "Yes. React detects changes by comparing references; `push` mutates the same array and `setTodos(todos)` passes the same reference, so React may skip the re-render, and memoised children won't update. Create a new array:\n\n```jsx\nsetTodos(prev => [...prev, t]);\n```\n\nSame for objects: `setUser(u => ({ ...u, name }))`. Treat state as read-only.", diff="medium")
d("react_state", "useState vs useReducer - which should I use?", "Use `useState` for simple, independent values. Switch to `useReducer` when state has several related fields, when the next state depends on complex rules, or when many different actions update it. A reducer centralises the transitions in a pure function that is easy to test:\n\n```jsx\nfunction reducer(state, action) {\n  switch (action.type) {\n    case 'add': return { ...state, items: [...state.items, action.item] };\n    case 'clear': return { ...state, items: [] };\n    default: return state;\n  }\n}\nconst [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { items: [] });\n```", diff="medium")
d("react_hooks", "Can I call hooks inside an if statement?", "No. Hooks must be called in the same order on every render, at the top level of a component or custom hook, not inside conditions, loops or nested functions. React tracks hooks by call order, so a skipped call corrupts the state mapping. Put the condition inside the hook instead:\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!enabled) return;\n  // ...\n}, [enabled]);\n```", diff="easy")
d("react_hooks", "How do I write a custom hook, e.g. for a debounced value?", "A custom hook is a function whose name starts with `use` and which calls other hooks:\n\n```jsx\nfunction useDebounced(value, ms) {\n  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), ms);\n    return () => clearTimeout(id);\n  }, [value, ms]);\n  return debounced;\n}\n```\n\nUse it as `const q = useDebounced(input, 300);` and fetch when `q` changes. The cleanup function cancels the previous timer, which is what makes it a debounce.", diff="medium")
d("react_hooks", "When do I need a ref instead of state?", "Use a ref for values that must persist across renders but whose change shouldn't cause a re-render: DOM nodes (`ref={inputRef}`), timer ids, the previous value, or a mutable instance variable. Use state for anything the UI displays. Don't read or write `ref.current` during rendering for display logic; do it in event handlers and effects.", diff="medium")
d("react_forms", "How do I submit a form without a page reload in React?", "Handle `onSubmit` on the `<form>` and call `preventDefault()`:\n\n```jsx\nfunction Login() {\n  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');\n  async function onSubmit(e) {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    await fetch('/api/login', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ email }),\n    });\n  }\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\n      <input type=\"email\" value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} required />\n      <button type=\"submit\">Sign in</button>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nSubmitting via the form (not a button `onClick`) also makes the Enter key work. Never put a password in the URL or log it.", diff="easy")
d("react_security", "Is dangerouslySetInnerHTML really dangerous?", "It bypasses React's automatic escaping, so any user-controlled HTML can run script in your page (XSS). Prefer rendering text normally with `{value}`, which React escapes. If you must render user-supplied HTML (for example Markdown output), sanitise it with a well-maintained sanitiser such as DOMPurify before inserting it, and add a Content-Security-Policy as a second layer.", diff="medium")
d("react_arch", "How should I structure a growing React project?", "Organise by feature rather than by file type: a folder per feature (`orders/`, `auth/`) containing its components, hooks, API calls and tests, plus a small `shared/` for generic UI. Keep components focused, move data fetching into hooks or a query library, and avoid prop drilling by lifting state sensibly or using context for truly global concerns (theme, current user). Enforce boundaries with lint rules so features don't import each other's internals.", diff="hard")
d("react_testing", "How should I test a React component?", "Test behaviour from the user's point of view with React Testing Library: render the component, interact as a user would, and assert on what is visible.\n\n```jsx\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';\nimport userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';\n\ntest('adds a todo', async () => {\n  render(<Todos />);\n  await userEvent.type(screen.getByLabelText(/new todo/i), 'milk');\n  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add/i }));\n  expect(screen.getByText('milk')).toBeInTheDocument();\n});\n```\n\nQuery by role and label, which also encourages accessible markup, and avoid testing internal state or implementation details.", diff="medium")
d("react_hooks", "Review this component:\n\n```jsx\nfunction Profile({ userId }) {\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\n  fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);\n  return <div>{user.name}</div>;\n}\n```", "Several problems:\n\n1. The `fetch` runs during render, so it fires on every render, and `setUser` triggers another render: an infinite request loop.\n2. `user` is `null` on the first render, so `user.name` throws.\n3. No error or loading state.\n\nMove the request into an effect keyed on `userId`, and render defensively:\n\n```jsx\nfunction Profile({ userId }) {\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    let cancelled = false;\n    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)\n      .then(r => r.json())\n      .then(u => { if (!cancelled) setUser(u); });\n    return () => { cancelled = true; };\n  }, [userId]);\n  if (!user) return <p>Loading...</p>;\n  return <div>{user.name}</div>;\n}\n```", diff="medium")
d("react_state", "What is the difference between props and state?", "Props are inputs passed into a component by its parent; the component treats them as read-only. State is data the component owns and can change over time, with a setter that triggers a re-render. A good rule: if a value can be computed from props or other state, don't store it in state; compute it during render.", diff="easy")
d("react_arch", "What is context good for, and when is it a bad idea?", "Context passes values through the tree without threading props, which suits things many components read and rarely change: theme, locale, the signed-in user. Every consumer re-renders when the context value changes, so it is a poor fit for rapidly changing data (such as input text or mouse position) or a large state object that many components use for different parts. Split contexts by concern, memoise the provider value, or use a state library with selectors when updates are frequent.", diff="hard")
d("react_hooks", "Why is my useEffect running twice in development?", "In React's Strict Mode (the default in many dev setups), React deliberately mounts, unmounts and remounts components in development to expose effects that lack proper cleanup. It doesn't happen in production builds. The fix is not to disable Strict Mode but to make the effect resilient: return a cleanup function that undoes the setup (cancel the request, close the connection, clear the timer) so a second run is harmless.", diff="medium")
d("react_perf", "My list of 10,000 rows is slow. What do I do?", "Rendering thousands of DOM nodes is the bottleneck. Options, in order of impact: virtualise the list so only visible rows are in the DOM (react-window or TanStack Virtual); paginate or filter on the server; keep row components cheap and memoised with stable keys; avoid creating new inline objects or functions per row. Measure with the browser's performance panel and the React Profiler before and after.", diff="hard")
d("react_state", "How do I share state across routes after login?", "Keep the authenticated user in a top-level provider (context) or a small store, populated when the app loads (for example by calling a session endpoint) and updated after login or logout. Protect routes with a wrapper component that redirects when there is no user. Remember this only controls the UI: the server must still enforce authorisation on every request, since a user can change client code.", diff="medium")
d("react_forms", "Controlled or uncontrolled input for a big form with 30 fields?", "For many fields, an uncontrolled approach avoids re-rendering the whole form on every keystroke. Use a form library (such as React Hook Form) or read values from `FormData` on submit:\n\n```jsx\nfunction onSubmit(e) {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  const data = Object.fromEntries(new FormData(e.currentTarget));\n  save(data);\n}\n```\n\nUse controlled inputs where you need live validation or dependent fields. Always validate again on the server.", diff="hard")
d("react_hooks", "What is the cleanup function in useEffect for?", "It runs before the effect re-runs and when the component unmounts. Use it to undo what the effect set up: remove event listeners, clear timers, abort requests, close sockets, unsubscribe.\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const onResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);\n  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);\n  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);\n}, []);\n```\n\nWithout the cleanup, each mount adds another listener and you leak memory.", diff="easy")

